>n

The Landing Meadow
A wide, flat meadow of soft grass where visitors, on the rare occasions that
Lamuella has any, are invited to park. The sky above it is very large and has
the look of somewhere things can arrive from. The village green is back to the
south.

The pilot dozes against the skiff's flank, cap over his eyes. 

A mail skiff squats in the grass, its engine ticking as it cools. 

Ford ambles in after you, towel over one shoulder.

>give sandwich to pilot
The pilot eats it in four bites, eyes closed, like a man receiving a sacrament.
"I'd fly you to the edge of the Galaxy for that," he says. "Well. Not the edge.
But somewhere." He taps the skiff's flank. "Whenever you're ready."

[The score has just gone up by ten points.]

>enter skiff

The pilot checks nothing in particular, says a word to the instrument panel, and
the world drops out from under you. There is a sensation like missing a step in
the dark, followed by another like finding it. For one long instant in the
middle of the jump you think of a girl who stepped into one of these and was not
on the other side; you grip the armrest until the feeling passes.

"Not long now," says Ford, mostly to himself. "Earth. The one that's still
there."

A London Park, Cordoned Off
A wet green London park bisected by blue-and-white tape. Beyond it, reporters
stamp in the cold and a dozen cameras have been turned toward the middle of the
lawn, where something has landed. It looks like an enormous bulk tin of
something, with a door. A pub sign swings to the east.

Ford is here, restless, taking in the room as though pricing it. 

Random lounges against the wall with her arms folded, looking at everything the
way a cat looks at a bath. 

The pilot has gone back to sleep against the skiff. 

A mail skiff has been parked, with serene disregard, where no parking is
allowed. 

Random is already here, standing a little too close to the woman with the
microphone and looking at her with an intensity that has only one explanation.
"Mum?" she says.

"Er," says the woman, "no." Her face, in profile, is a face you would know in
the dark. It is the face of the woman who left you on Lamuella half an hour ago,
only younger, and with the particular wariness of someone who has never left
home.

[The score has just gone up by five points.]

>e

The Dancing Gnu
A pub that has been trying to look historic since about last Tuesday. Low beams,
dark wood, a carpet whose pattern has given up. The door is to the west. At a
small corner table, a man in a damp raincoat drinks alone, with the look of
someone who is waiting for something to happen, again.

Ford ambles in after you, towel over one shoulder.

>wait
Time passes.

Tricia comes in with Random in tow, steering her toward a table.

>wait
Time passes.

Tricia gets a round in. Random sits with her bag in her lap, looking at
everything and trusting nothing. Something in her coat pocket makes a faint,
contented purring.

>wait
Time passes.

Random takes something short and ugly out of her bag and puts it on the table,
where it looks out of place. It is a laser pistol. She has found it somewhere on
the way and has not yet read the instructions.

>wait
Time passes.

>wait
Time passes.

Random picks up the pistol to hand it to you, and her thumb finds the one
control she hasn't read about. There is a click, a whine, and a line of white
light that makes the air smell of toast.

The beam wanders. It touches the beams, a bottle, the corner table. A man in a
damp raincoat is no longer sitting at it. The pub erupts: there is shouting, a
stampede, a barman diving for the cellar, someone on a table.

